  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The $\\chi^2$ distribution arises from sums of squared, normally distributed, random variables — if $x_i \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$, then $u = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 \\sim \\chi_n^2$, a central $\\chi^2$ distribution with $n$ degrees of freedom. It follows that the sum of two $\\chi^2$ random variables is also $\\chi^2$ distributed, so that if $u \\sim \\chi_n^2$ and $v \\sim \\chi_m^2$, then",
      "source_page": 890,
      "printed_page": 870,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p890:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        143.0,
        538.0,
        1102.0,
        685.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula297]]",
      "source_page": 890,
      "printed_page": 870,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p890:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        529.0,
        700.0,
        719.0,
        743.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Two other useful results are that if $ x_{i} \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0, \\sigma^{2}) $, then",
      "source_page": 890,
      "printed_page": 870,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p890:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        148.0,
        768.0,
